430:
It is the purpose of this article to promote, protect, maintain and enhance the short-term and long-term public health, safety and general welfare and to minimize public and private losses due to flood conditions in specific areas by provisions designed to:
(1) Restrict or prohibit uses which are dangerous to health, safety and property due to water or erosion, flood heights or velocities;
(2) Require that uses vulnerable to floods, including facilities which serve such uses, be protected against flood damages at the time of initial construction;
(3) Control the alteration of natural floodplains, stream channels, wetlands, and natural protective barriers which are involved in the accommodation of floodwaters;
(4) Prevent or regulate the construction of flood barriers which will unnaturally divert floodwaters or which may increase flood hazards to other lands; and
(5) Comply with state (DNR) and federal (EPA) stormwater regulations developed pursuant to the Clean Water Act. These requirements include:
a. Control the contribution of pollutants to the storm sewer system by stormwater discharges associated with commercial and industrial activity and the quality of stormwater discharged from sites of commercial and industrial activity;
b. Prohibit illicit connections to storm sewers;
c. Control discharge to storm sewers of spills, dumping or disposal of materials other than stormwater; and
d. Control, through intergovernmental agreements, contribution of pollutants from one city system to another.
(Ord. No. 97-1, § 3, 1-28-1997)

432:
The enactment and enforcement of this article is intended to address the following policy objectives:
(1) To recognize the necessity of and to encourage joint action and a full exchange of information by the city and the development industry in resolution of existing drainage problems, the prevention of their worsening or recurrence and the implementation of these objectives;
(2) To work in a cooperative relationship with the development industry to accomplish the objective stated in subsection (1) of this section by encouraging through this article and other public actions the development of on-site self-maintaining retention/detention facilities and for off-site centrally located regional retention/detention facilities;
(3) To maintain, where feasible, the natural environment of city streams through reduction of flow quantities resulting from new development and, where feasible, restore the floodplain to its natural functional purpose to resolve existing flooding problems, but recognize the necessity of using urban streams for stormwater runoff in those situations where maintenance of the natural stream environment would result in continuation or worsening of flooding conditions;
(4) To incrementally assume maintenance responsibility for the public drainage system;
(5) To require of the development industry the responsibility to maintain the functioning of the storm drainage system;
(6) To encourage the construction of joint retention/detention ponds serving several properties which can be accepted for maintenance by the city upon completion of project development. The city encourages (where engineering judgment so warrants) the location of these retention facilities off the site of the property under development and may participate in the construction of major retention facilities which would serve not only the needs of the property under development, but would assist in resolving existing flooding problems;
(7) To encourage innovative design solutions to the effective retention and reduction of runoff. Drainage control measures to retain, reduce and regulate the rate of stormwater discharge which shall be acceptable shall include, but need not be limited to, retention/detention ponds, percolation facilities, parking lots, open space areas, and oversized storm drains with restricted outlets. In addition, the city encourages the development of retention facilities in such a manner that they will become aesthetically and recreationally beneficial to the community in which they are located; and
(8) To require any development to retain any runoff which exceeds an allowable increase of one cubic foot per second per acre caused by the development and to utilize the parking area or privately owned retention/detention facility.
(Ord. No. 97-1, § 6, 1-28-1997)

435:
The liberty consolidated planning commission will administer and enforce the provisions of this article.
(1) The liberty consolidated planning commission shall:
a. Review all development permits to ensure that the permit requirements of this article have been satisfied;
b. Advise permittee when additional federal or state permits may be required; and, if specific federal or state permits are known to be required, ensure that copies of such permits are provided and maintained on file with the development permit;
c. Notify adjacent communities, the Federal Emergency Management Agency and the state department of natural resources prior to any alteration or relocation of a watercourse and submit evidence of such notification to the Federal Insurance Administration;
d. Administer, coordinate and oversee acquisition, design, construction, and operations and maintenance (O&M) of municipal/county stormwater facilities and conveyances;
e. Establish or oversee establishment of development standards and guidelines;
f. Determine the manner in which stormwater facilities should be operated;
g. Inspect private systems which discharge to the county and city storm sewer systems;
h. Advise the city and other departments on issues related to stormwater;
i. Protect facilities and properties controlled by the city and prescribe how they are used by others;
j. Require new, increased, or significantly changed stormwater contributions to comply with the terms of this article;
k. Develop programs or procedures to control the discharge of pollutants into the city storm sewer system.
(2) The liberty consolidated planning commission or his/her representative shall administer and enforce those provisions of this article which apply to developed and occupied areas and to undeveloped areas which affect the city's responsibility for maintenance of the storm drainage system. The liberty consolidated planning commission shall ensure that any altered or relocated portion of any watercourse is maintained so that flood-carrying capacity is not diminished.
(Ord. No. 97-1, § 40, 1-28-1997)

